    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d102">

                <pb facs="0240" n="128" sameAs="frus:frus1910/pg128"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 566/13.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Chargé Harvey.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1910-02-09"><hi rendition="#italic">February 9,
                        1910</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 26, Bulgarian series.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The department has received and read with
                    interest your No. 49, Bulgarian series, of the 25th ultimo,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d102-f1"/> relative to the trip which you took to Sofia for the
                    purpose of ascertaining the exact attitude of the Bulgarian authorities with
                    reference to the collection of a supplemental income tax from foreigners engaged
                    in business in Bulgaria, and its effect on American merchants and agents
                    there.</p>
                <p>It appears from your dispatch that several of the powers have opposed the
                    collection of this tax for the reason that the capitulations existing under the
                    Turkish régime are still in force in Bulgaria, <pb facs="0241" n="129"
                        xml:id="pg129"/> and therefore the Bulgarian Government would appear to have
                    no right to enforce the collection of any new taxes upon foreign residents
                    without the consent of their respective governments.</p>
                <p>The department approves the representations made by you to the Bulgarian minister
                    for foreign affairs, namely, that American citizens be accorded the same
                    treatment given to other foreigners engaged in business in Bulgaria, and that
                    British consular officers be permitted to continue to act in the interests of
                    American citizens.</p>
                <p>The courtesy extended to you by Mr. Lindley, His Britannic Majesty’s chargé
                    d’affaires at Sofia, who accompanied you to the foreign office and who on
                    several occasions has interfered to protect American citizens, is greatly
                    appreciated, and the embassy at London has been instructed suitably to express
                    to the foreign office the appreciation felt by this department and to ask that
                    its thanks may be conveyed to Mr. Lindley for his services.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">P. C. Knox.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
